WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Formal curricula can help organize auditory training, but their structures differ.

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:12.205
DASL Two uses brief individualized sessions and emphasizes sound awareness, phonetic listening, and auditory comprehension.

00:00:13.085 --> 00:00:21.085
SPICE has four named categories: detection, suprasegmental perception, vowel and consonant perception, and connected speech.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:29.530
SKI-HI emphasizes infant home intervention with the family, combining auditory stimulation with language and speech stimulation.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.395
The course lists four phases and eleven general skills.

00:00:34.235 --> 00:00:39.915
The Cottage Acquisition Scales integrate listening, language, cognition, and speech.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:47.670
Their age range differs between the supplied slide and the automatic transcript, so the lesson keeps that discrepancy visible.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.080
Learn to Talk Around the Clock emphasizes caregiver interaction in everyday home activities.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.220
Choose a program that fits the child's and family's assessed needs.

00:00:58.895 --> 00:01:04.255
Shared listening principles do not mean identical categories, targets, or pacing.

00:01:04.935 --> 00:01:09.975
A curriculum supplies resources; the individual child still determines the plan.

